course: everyday
lesson: 103
title: Screens, sleep, fairness and differences
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Screens are fun, but too much screen time can make you grumpy and spoil your sleep, so families agree on limits and children take breaks outside.
explain: Bright screens keep your brain awake, which is why a book or calm sounds work better at bedtime.
explain: Fair play, real compliments and respect for people who are different from you make others want to be with you.
explain: For example, a boy in Toronto whose classmate uses a wheelchair treats her like anyone else, and asks «Would you like help?» before pushing her chair.
fact: Too much screen time can affect your mood and your sleep.
fact: Taking breaks, going outside and sticking to agreed limits help you feel better.
fact: If you see something upsetting online, close it and tell a trusted adult.
fact: Many children your age do not have a phone, and every family decides when it is right.
fact: Screens before bed keep your brain awake, so reading or calm sounds help you fall asleep.
fact: Sharing your sweets kindly is good, but it is your choice.
fact: Cheating spoils a game, and people will not want to play with a cheat.
fact: When someone praises you, the polite answer is «thank you».
fact: A good compliment is specific and true.
fact: Classmates from different cultures or religions deserve curiosity and respect.
fact: People with disabilities want respect, not pity, so treat them as anyone else and ask before helping.
fact: Families come in many forms, and what matters is that they love each other.
exercise: You feel grumpy after playing video games all afternoon. What helps? | Take a break, go outside and keep to agreed limits | Play for another hour until you cheer up | Switch to a different screen | Stay up late playing to relax
exercise: You see a frightening video online. What should you do? | Close it and tell a trusted adult | Keep watching to see how it ends | Send it to a friend to ask about it | Keep it secret so you are not in trouble
exercise: Everyone in your class seems to have a phone except you. What is true? | Many children your age do not have one, and each family decides | Your parents must buy you one straight away | You are the only child without a phone | Without a phone you cannot have friends
exercise: You cannot fall asleep at night. What is most likely to help? | Put the screens away and read or listen to calm sounds | Watch videos in bed until you feel tired | Play a fast game to tire your brain | Turn on all the lights and wait
exercise: Why do screens make it hard to fall asleep? | They keep your brain awake | They make your bed too warm | They use up the air in the room | They make you hungry at night
exercise: Your brother asks for half of your sweets. What is true? | Sharing is kind, but it is your choice | You must always give him exactly half | You must never share your sweets | He can take them if he is younger
exercise: You could peek at the other player's cards and win. What should you do? | Play fairly and not peek | Peek just this once | Peek only if you are losing badly | Peek, since everyone cheats sometimes
exercise: Why do children stop playing with someone who cheats? | Cheating spoils the game for everyone | Cheats are always better players | Cheats always win in the end | Cheating is only bad in school games
exercise: Your teacher says, «Great drawing!» What is the polite thing to say? | Thank you | No, it is really bad | I know, I am the best | Nothing, just look away
exercise: Which is the best compliment for a friend's story? | I loved the part where the dragon got lost | Your story was fine I guess | Your story beat everyone else's | Nice, can we play now
exercise: A new classmate in Sydney celebrates a religious festival you do not know. What is a good reaction? | Be curious and ask about it respectfully | Laugh at it because it is different | Tell her your festivals are much better | Ignore her because she is different
exercise: A classmate uses a wheelchair. How do you treat him? | Like anyone else, asking before you help | Push his chair without asking | Feel sorry for him all the time | Leave him out of games to keep him safe
exercise: A friend says she has two dads. What is true? | Families come in many forms | Every family must have a mum and a dad | She must have made a mistake | Her family is not a real family
test: Ella in Denver gets cross and tired every evening after hours on her tablet. What change would help most? | Shorter screen time and more time outside | A bigger tablet with a brighter screen so it is easier on her eyes | Playing on her tablet in bed instead | Skipping dinner to finish her games
test: Which is true about upsetting things online? | A trusted adult can help you understand it and stay safe | You should keep on watching it until you are not scared any more | It is your fault if you see something bad | Telling an adult always gets you in trouble
test: Josh wins a board game by moving his piece when nobody is looking. What is the likely result? | His friends do not want to play with him again | His friends think he is very clever | He becomes the best player for real | Nobody ever minds about it
test: Why is «You are great» a weaker compliment than «You were so patient helping me with maths»? | A specific, true compliment means more | Short compliments are always rude | Long compliments are never believed by anybody | Talking about maths is not allowed
test: Why should you ask a person with a disability before helping them? | They want respect, and may not need or want help | They are never allowed to be helped | Asking first makes them feel sorry for themselves and sad | People with disabilities never need help
test: Which is the best way to get to sleep on a school night? | A calm story in bed with the screens put away | A loud game until you feel sleepy | A scary film to tire you out | A bright phone screen under the covers until your eyes are tired
test: Your friend shares only some of her crisps with you. What is a fair reaction? | Say thank you, as it is her choice | Tell her that she must give you exactly half | Take the rest when she looks away | Tell the teacher she is mean
test: A boy in Leeds says «Your family is weird» because a classmate has two mums. What is true? | What matters is that a family loves each other | Families with two mums are not real | The classmate should keep her family a secret from everyone at school | Only families like his are normal
test: saw something online that really scared me | Close it and tell a trusted adult | Keep watching to understand it | Send it to your friends | Never go online again and tell no one
test: everyones got a phone in my class but me, not fair | Many kids your age do not have one; each family decides | Everyone else has one, so your parents must get you one now | Your family does not care about you | Get one secretly without asking
test: she said "nice drawing" and i said "nah its rubbish" | The polite answer is thank you | Say yes, it is better than yours | Say nothing and look away | Tell her hers is rubbish too
test: man in a wheelchair, should i just push him up the ramp | Ask before helping | Yes, push him without asking | Yes, he must need help | No, never offer him any help
explain: A boy in Ohio played on screens until late every night; his sleep, marks and mood got worse until his family set screen-free time before bed.
explain: A girl in Sydney clicked a link in a game promising free coins; it stole her mum's account password, and they lost money and the account.
fact: Links offering free coins, skins (new looks for game characters) or prizes are often scams, tricks that steal accounts or money.
fact: Too much screen time, especially late at night, makes it harder to sleep and concentrate.
test: link says free 1000 coins just log in | Don't click, it's a scam to steal your account | Click it fast before the offer ends, free coins are free coins | Give your password, it's safe | Share it with all your friends
test: playing till midnight every night, its fine | Stop earlier, late screens wreck your sleep | It's fine as long as the screen is turned down dim | You need less sleep with games | Only weekends count
test: kid in a wheelchair joined our class, my mate says don't play with her | Include her, being different is fine | Leave her out, she's different | Only play when teachers watch | Ask her to just watch from the side while you all play
test: did u hear Mason clicked a free skins link n his dad lost like a million dollars n they lost their house lol. what's probably exaggerated | The million and the house; scam links do steal accounts and money | That links offering free skins can be scams | That clicking a link can lose someone an account | Nothing, it is all true, because scam links always take away whole houses
test: my mate says gaming till 2am every night made some kid fail all his exams n his mum took the xbox. what's the real lesson | Late screen time spoils sleep and concentration, so keep limits | Gaming late every night is fine as long as you sleep in at the weekend | Only exams matter, so screens are fine in the holidays | Screens before bed help the brain wind down for sleep
test: Priya's family don't eat pork n Josh is calling it weird, tellin everyone. should i join in | No, different cultures deserve curiosity and respect | Yes, because her family's rules are strange | Yes, but only when Priya is not listening | Yes, so she learns that she has to fit in with everyone else
exam: Identify the principal reason for which reading is preferred to screens immediately before sleep. | The light of screens maintains the brain in a wakeful state | Books contain no words that are capable of exciting the reader | Screens consume electricity whereas books do not | Reading is required by law in many households
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a link offering free coins, skins or prizes within a game? | It is frequently a fraud designed to steal accounts or money | It is invariably a genuine gift from the makers of the game | It is safe provided that a parent's password is entered into it | It is dangerous solely upon a mobile telephone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning assistance to a person who uses a wheelchair. | One asks before helping and treats the person as anyone else | One pushes the chair at once without asking | One avoids speaking to the person at all so as to give them space | One offers pity because the person needs it most
exam: Pursuant to encountering upsetting material online, ascertain the appropriate course of action. | Close the material and inform a trusted adult | Share the material so that friends are warned | Continue viewing it in order to understand it | Remain silent so as not to lose access to the device
